From: MilkFlint <milkflint@localhost>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 16:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net/sched: sch_cake: the bulk rotation in constant time

A bulk flow with no deficit left went to the tail of the bulk rotation
with its host's share of the bulk quantum, and was visited again on each
pass until its deficit was positive. A host's flows share one quantum,
so a packet of length L from a host with n bulk flows took about
n * L / quantum visits, each reading the host loads, drawing a random
number to dither the share and moving the flow, all within one dequeue
under the qdisc lock. With GRO packets (no split-gso) that is several
visits a packet for a host with a dozen flows, and far more for one with
a hundred.

The rotation is now a wheel of 32 slots, one for each pass to come. A
flow out of deficit gets at once the shares of the passes it needs, up to
31, and waits in the slot of the pass it would next have sent in. The
slot being served is the head of the rotation; once it's empty, the next
slot with flows is, and its pass begins. The flows of a pass are served
in the order they joined it, and a flow with deficit left keeps its place
at the head of its slot, as on the list. A flow is visited about once a
packet, or once every 31 passes where a packet takes more.

The deficit is kept in fixed point (bytes << 13), so a host's share of a
quantum keeps its fraction instead of being dithered to whole bytes. A GSO
packet of over 128 KB (BIG TCP's) costs its flow's deficit 128 KB, as
much as the s32 has room for; the shaper still charges all of it. The
class dump still reports bytes.

What changes: the host load is read when a flow is put in its slot, not
on each pass, so a change in a host's flow count reaches a waiting flow
up to 31 passes late; and a pass serves its flows in the order they
joined it, not in the list's. Shares over time are the same.

KUnit: the wheel's workings; the same flows through the wheel and through
a copy of the list rotation (one host's flow against another's sixteen,
sixteen flows alone, a hundred alone, a host going from sixteen flows to
one; GRO-sized and MTU-sized packets): at every point each flow's bytes
within what it sends in a pass of the list's, more where a host's flow
count fell, the same rates once settled, within 1% of the dithered
shares, and at most two visits a packet, or one every 31 passes; and two
hosts' flows through cake_enqueue() and cake_dequeue().

